I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text'. Reply 1 is the string ` ```json\n{"a": 1} extra\n```\n{"b": 2}`. Reply 2 is the string `{"b": 2}`. For Reply 1, the rule requires the answer `{"a": 1}`, because the code fence holds the object `{"a": 1}` and that is the last object inside a fence. For Reply 2, the rule requires the answer `{"b": 2}`, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is `{"b": 2}`. The check gives the same answer for both: it returns `{"b": 2}` for Reply 1, because the fenced candidate `\n{"a": 1} extra\n` fails to parse as a whole, so the function falls through to the top-level pool and takes the last top-level object, which is `{"b": 2}`. Thus the difference the rule requires is lost. I would be wrong if recover_json_object(Reply 1) actually returns `{"a": 1}`, or if the rule is read to require that a fenced block must parse as a single JSON object before it counts as holding one.